{
  "id": "06-first-missing-positive",
  "title": "First Missing Positive",
  "track": "Arrays",
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "topics": [
    "arrays"
  ],
  "source": "canonical First Missing Positive concept; description, examples, and tests re-authored",
  "description": "The input is an array of integers that may contain negatives, zeros, and duplicates. Return the smallest positive integer that does not appear anywhere in the array. The answer is always 1 or greater.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= len(nums) <= 5000",
    "-1000000000 <= nums[i] <= 1000000000"
  ],
  "parameters": [
    "nums"
  ],
  "functionName": "first_missing_positive",
  "starterCode": "def first_missing_positive(nums):\n    # Return the smallest positive integer missing from nums.\n    ...\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": {
        "nums": [
          2,
          3,
          -4,
          1,
          7
        ]
      },
      "output": 4,
      "explanation": "The values 1, 2, 3 all appear, so the smallest missing positive is 4."
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "nums": [
          8,
          9,
          10
        ]
      },
      "output": 1,
      "explanation": "No positive value below 8 appears, so the answer is 1."
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "The answer always lies between 1 and len(nums) + 1: if every position held its ideal value, the answer would be len(nums) + 1.",
    "An array position can stand for a value. If value v (with 1 <= v <= n) sat at index v - 1, you could tell in one sweep which value is homeless.",
    "Swap each value v in the range 1..n to index v - 1, repeating the swap at the same index until it holds something useless. Then scan for the first index i where nums[i] != i + 1; that missing value is i + 1."
  ],
  "solution": "Cyclic sort: repeatedly swap each in-range value v to index v - 1 until no swap applies, which places every value 1..n that exists into its home slot in O(n) total swaps. A final sweep returns the first slot whose value is wrong; if all match, the answer is n + 1. O(n) time and O(1) extra space when the rearrangement happens in place.",
  "solutionCode": "def first_missing_positive(nums):\n    values = list(nums)\n    n = len(values)\n    for i in range(n):\n        while 1 <= values[i] <= n and values[values[i] - 1] != values[i]:\n            target = values[i] - 1\n            values[i], values[target] = values[target], values[i]\n    for i in range(n):\n        if values[i] != i + 1:\n            return i + 1\n    return n + 1\n",
  "bruteForceCode": "def first_missing_positive(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    for candidate in range(1, n + 2):\n        found = False\n        for value in nums:\n            if value == candidate:\n                found = True\n                break\n        if not found:\n            return candidate\n    return n + 1\n",
  "failureHint": "hidden tests include duplicates, all-negative arrays, and a complete 1..n run",
  "expectedComplexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(1)"
  },
